            "<strong>Intellectual (learning) disability</strong> is significantly reduced intellectual functioning and adaptive skills beginning in the developmental period; it is graded mild (most — able to live independently with support, often found only when school begins), moderate, severe and profound. Its causes in Ghana are mostly preventable: <strong>birth asphyxia</strong>, severe neonatal jaundice (kernicterus), prematurity, <strong>meningitis</strong> and <strong>cerebral malaria</strong>, <strong>iodine deficiency</strong> in the mother (the reason salt is iodised), fetal alcohol exposure, malnutrition, and genetic conditions, of which <strong>Down syndrome</strong> is the commonest — recognisable at birth and associated with heart defects, hypothyroidism, hearing loss and early dementia, all of which need screening. <strong>Cerebral palsy</strong> is a disorder of movement and posture from an injury to the developing brain (asphyxia, jaundice, infection, prematurity); it may or may not come with intellectual disability, and the child who cannot speak or walk may understand everything said over her head. <strong>Autism</strong> is a difference in social communication (eye contact, pointing, sharing interest, conversation) with restricted, repetitive interests and behaviour and often sensory sensitivity, present from early childhood; it ranges from the non-speaking child with intellectual disability to the able adolescent whose literal thinking and distress at change go unrecognised.",
            "<strong>Health care for a person with an intellectual disability or autism</strong>: speak to her, not only to the carer, simply and slowly, with pictures, demonstration and time; involve the carer who knows how she shows pain and fear; keep the environment quiet and predictable, the same nurse where possible, and explain each step before it happens; allow the familiar object or routine; assess capacity for each decision rather than assuming its absence, and where she lacks it act in her best interests with the family; and remember that <strong>pain and illness present as a change in behaviour</strong> — new aggression, self-injury, withdrawal or refusal to eat is toothache, otitis, constipation or a fracture until proved otherwise, and <strong>diagnostic overshadowing</strong> kills people with intellectual disability as surely as it kills people with schizophrenia. In many communities a disabled child is hidden, blamed on the mother or a curse, or in some northern areas has been regarded as a 'spirit child' and killed; <strong>early identification</strong> at child welfare clinics, honest explanation that nobody is to blame, inclusive schooling and community-based rehabilitation (Lesson 7.2) are what change that.",
