            "<strong>Epilepsy</strong> is a tendency to recurrent unprovoked seizures — sudden, excessive electrical discharges in the brain. In Ghana it affects perhaps 1 in 100 people, is managed largely by community psychiatric nurses within the mental health programme, carries a stigma as heavy as any psychosis (it is believed to be contagious through saliva, to be caused by spirits or witchcraft, to bar marriage and school), and has a treatment gap as wide as that for mental illness although it is one of the cheapest conditions in medicine to control. Causes include <strong>birth asphyxia</strong>, <strong>cerebral malaria</strong>, meningitis and encephalitis, head injury, stroke, tumours, neurocysticercosis, alcohol, and genetics; often none is found. <strong>Generalised tonic-clonic seizures</strong> — a cry, stiffening, falling, jerking of all limbs, cyanosis, tongue-biting and incontinence, then a post-ictal sleep and confusion of minutes to hours; <strong>absences</strong> in children — brief blank spells mistaken for daydreaming and poor attention; and <strong>focal seizures</strong> — an aura, automatisms such as lip-smacking and fumbling, or jerking of one limb, with or without impaired awareness, sometimes with fear, strange smells or déjà vu that are misread as psychiatric. <strong>Febrile convulsions</strong> — brief generalised seizures with fever in children of six months to five years — are usually benign and are not epilepsy, but the fever's cause (malaria, meningitis) must be found.",
